     "## Discussion\n",
     "\n",
-    "Natural language processing-based models proved to be a powerful tool for a wide range of molecular tasks. In this talktorial we introduced the basics of Recurrent Neural Network architectures and demonstrated their application to the regression task on the QM9 dataset. We have learned how to pre-process SMILES strings, build a model using PyTorch, and train the model to predict the dipole moment of the molecule. "
+    "Natural language processing techniques have found applications in a wide range of cheminformatics tasks. In this talktorial, we introduced the fundamentals of recurrent neural networks (RNNs) and demonstrated their application to molecular property prediction using the QM9 dataset. We explored how to preprocess SMILES strings, implement and train RNN-based models in PyTorch, and evaluate their performance in predicting molecular dipole moments.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "To keep the examples simple and computationally inexpensive, we used simplified model architectures, fixed hyperparameters, and random data splitting. For real-world applications, model architectures and hyperparameters such as the hidden-state dimension, number of recurrent layers, learning rate, and batch size can be optimized for better performance. Readers can try different configurations by changing the model parameters and training settings above and comparing their performance on the validation set. For an overview of hyperparameter tuning strategies, see [Bischl et al. (2023)](https://doi.org/10.1002/widm.1484)."
    ]
